# radiant_core - an independent link layer compatible with the ANT+
# specifications. Not ANT, not Garmin's, and named so that nothing about the
# build surface reads as a claim to be either.

config RADIANT_CORE
	bool "radiant_core ANT-compatible link layer"
	help
	  Build radiant_core: the frame codec, the radio schedule, the channel
	  state machines, the search policy, the event queue and the
	  acknowledged/burst transfer engine.

	  This is the module the application selects with -DANT_RADIO=core. It
	  contains no Nordic sdk-ant code and links no libant.a; an sdk_ant
	  build leaves this symbol at n, and that n is the positive assertion
	  that the clean-room boundary held.

if RADIANT_CORE

module = RADIANT_CORE
module-str = radiant_core
source "subsys/logging/Kconfig.template.log_config"

choice RADIANT_CORE_BACKEND
	prompt "radiant_core radio backend"
	default RADIANT_CORE_BACKEND_NULL
	help
	  radiant_core decides what goes on the air and when; a backend decides
	  how. include/radiant_core/radiant_radio_hal.h is the whole of that
	  boundary and its eight entry points are what a backend supplies.

	  A backend REPLACES the others rather than joining them: two
	  definitions of radiant_radio_now() is a duplicate-symbol link error,
	  which is the right way for that mistake to be found.

config RADIANT_CORE_BACKEND_NULL
	bool "null - real lifecycle and clock, refuses every arm"
	help
	  src/radiant_radio_null.c. The lifecycle and the clock are real and
	  every arm call is refused with RADIANT_RADIO_ENOTSUP. The HAL-level
	  counterpart of src/ant_radio_stub.c: the image enumerates, answers the
	  host, runs the scheduler, the search policy, the channel state
	  machines and the event queue, and finds no sensors.

	  This is the honest default while no real backend exists.

config RADIANT_CORE_BACKEND_NONE
	bool "none - the application supplies the HAL"
	help
	  The module compiles no backend at all and the eight HAL entry points
	  are left undefined for the surrounding application to define. This is
	  what the unit-test application selects: tests/fake_radio.c is a
	  complete HAL implementation with a virtual clock, and it belongs in a
	  test image and nowhere near a dongle.

config RADIANT_CORE_BACKEND_NRF
	bool "nrf - direct nRF RADIO peripheral"
	#
	# SOC_COMPATIBLE_*, NOT SOC_SERIES_*, and the difference is a whole
	# session. NCS v3.4.0 ships Zephyr 4.4, which renamed SOC_SERIES_NRF52X
	# to SOC_SERIES_NRF52 and SOC_SERIES_NRF54LX to SOC_SERIES_NRF54L. The
	# old names still EXIST as symbols in that tree, so nothing errors -
	# they are simply never set, this dependency is silently unmet, and
	# Kconfig falls back to the choice default. The result is a build that
	# accepted -DRADIANT_BACKEND=nrf, printed no error, and compiled the
	# null backend: an image that enumerates, answers the host, runs every
	# state machine and finds no sensors, which is indistinguishable from a
	# radio problem.
	#
	# The SOC_COMPATIBLE_ symbols are set in BOTH v3.2.4 and v3.4.0 and are
	# the ones meant for exactly this - "this part behaves like that family"
	# - rather than "this part is in that Kconfig directory". The C file
	# tests the same pair.
	#
	depends on SOC_COMPATIBLE_NRF52X || SOC_COMPATIBLE_NRF54LX
	depends on !BT
	select CLOCK_CONTROL
	select NRFX_GPPI
	help
	  src/radiant_radio_nrf.c: the RADIO, one TIMER and four (D)PPI
	  channels, owned outright. Receive and transmit.

	  The TIMER named by the radiant,radio-timer chosen node must have at
	  least five CC channels - t_sync capture, RX start, window close,
	  software now-capture and TX start. Receive-start and transmit-start
	  cannot share a compare, because a DPPI event may publish to only one
	  channel. On nRF52 that means TIMER3 or TIMER4.

	  The address arithmetic and packet configuration are measured on
	  nRF54L15 (Spike A). The nRF52840 is predicted to behave identically
	  and has not been confirmed. The ramp-up and turnaround figures are
	  from the datasheet, and the two t_sync calibration constants are
	  seeded from Zephyr's per-SoC BLE chain delays rather than measured on
	  this bench - see the warnings at the top of the file.

config RADIANT_CORE_BACKEND_NRF_IRQ_PRIO
	int "RADIO interrupt priority"
	depends on RADIANT_CORE_BACKEND_NRF
	default 0
	help
	  radiant_radio_hal.h says callbacks run "at the highest priority the
	  backend uses", because re-arming from inside a completion callback is
	  the only path that meets a 1.55 ms acknowledged-data turnaround.
	  Lower this only with a measurement in hand.

config RADIANT_CORE_BACKEND_MPSL
	bool "mpsl - RADIO through MPSL timeslots (Phase 6b, not written)"
	help
	  A backend arbitrating with the BLE controller through MPSL rather than
	  owning RADIO. Reserved here on the same terms as the nrf one.

endchoice

config RADIANT_CORE_ANTR_API
	bool "compile the antr_* adapter (src/radiant_api.c)"
	default y
	help
	  src/radiant_api.c is the integration layer: it implements the fifty
	  antr_* entry points that src/ant_radio.h declares, on top of the
	  modules below it. It is the only file in the module that reaches out
	  to an application header, so it is the only one an application-free
	  build has to be able to leave out.

	  The unit-test application sets this to n. Its suites test the modules,
	  not the forwarding, and compiling the adapter there would drag
	  src/ant_radio.h's whole surface into a build that has no bridge to
	  satisfy it.

config RADIANT_CORE_CRC_REPAIR
	bool "repair single-bit CRC errors on tracked windows"
	default n
	help
	  src/radiant_crc_repair.c. On a tracked window that failed its CRC,
	  form the error syndrome from the received CRC and the computed one,
	  look it up in a table of every single-bit error, and flip the bit
	  back. At the sensitivity knee essentially every CRC failure is one
	  flipped bit - around 136 exposed bits at a 1e-4 bit error rate puts
	  two-bit failures two orders down - so this recovers nearly all of
	  them, for a table and no change on the air. Worth roughly 1-3 dB of
	  effective sensitivity.

	  THE ARITHMETIC THAT DECIDES WHETHER THIS IS SAFE. A frame with more
	  bit errors than one can have its syndrome land on a valid single-bit
	  entry by chance, and is then "repaired" into a frame that is still
	  wrong and now claims to be right.

	  TWO-BIT ERRORS CANNOT. The polynomial x^16 + x^12 + x^5 + 1 is
	  divisible by (x + 1), so a syndrome's popcount carries the parity of
	  the error's weight: odd-weight errors have odd-popcount syndromes and
	  even-weight errors have even-popcount ones, and the two sets are
	  disjoint. Since two-bit errors are by far the commonest multi-bit case
	  at the knee, that removes most of the exposure outright - and
	  test_crc_repair.c enumerates all 3160 of them to keep it true.

	  What is left is odd-weight errors of three bits and up, which collide
	  at roughly 1 in 341 and are themselves another order of magnitude
	  rarer than two-bit ones. That figure is 96 REACHABLE entries against
	  32768 odd-popcount syndromes: a standard tracked frame has 80 body
	  bits and 16 CRC bits, and the rest of the table names positions that
	  would lie inside the address, which are refused because a flipped
	  address bit never matches the filter in the first place. The same
	  suite measures the rate rather than asserting it.

	  On a tracked window that is acceptable: the hardware matched a full
	  5-byte address, so the frame is already known to be from the right
	  sensor, and one bad payload is something every ANT+ profile survives
	  because its values are cumulative. On a SEARCH window it would not be,
	  which is why the module refuses that configuration outright rather
	  than trusting the call site - a search window's CRC failures are the
	  19-27 noise-triggered address matches per 15 seconds that
	  radiant_search.c counts and discards, and one in 585 of those would
	  become a device number manufactured out of thermal noise.

	  Requires a backend whose caps.has_rx_crc is true; the repair path is
	  inert without one, because the received CRC is not in the body and a
	  syndrome cannot be formed without it.

	  Default n. Repaired frames are counted separately in
	  radiant_api_stats.crc_repaired, so an A/B can see how many there were
	  and never confuses one for a clean receive.

config RADIANT_CORE_PROFILE_SANITY
	bool "drop a repaired frame whose payload is not physically possible"
	depends on RADIANT_CORE_CRC_REPAIR
	default n
	help
	  src/radiant_profile_sanity.c. A third refutation for a repaired
	  frame, alongside the control-byte and transmission-type checks
	  radiant_crc_repair.h documents: for a bicycle power meter
	  (device type 0x0B), reject an instantaneous power reading above
	  3000 W; for a heart rate monitor (device type 0x78), reject a
	  computed heart rate above 240 bpm. Both figures are comfortably
	  above real human extremes - see radiant_profile_sanity.h for why
	  the originally proposed 1000 W / 200 bpm would have misclassified
	  genuine efforts - so a payload this module rejects is far more
	  likely a mis-repair than a real reading.

	  APPLIED TO REPAIRED FRAMES ONLY, exactly like the two checks in
	  radiant_crc_repair.h: the call site in src/radiant_api.c makes this
	  the caller's rule to keep, not this module's, because the same
	  range check against a CLEAN frame would silently delete the most
	  interesting moments of a ride - a track sprint, a hard effort -
	  and look like a receiver fault.

	  This is profile knowledge (what a payload byte MEANS), which is
	  deliberately not part of radiant_crc_repair.c or any other
	  frame-layer module - see radiant_profile_sanity.h for why it has
	  its own file and its own symbol instead.

	  Rejections are counted in radiant_api_stats.crc_repair_implausible,
	  separately from crc_repair_refuted, so an A/B can tell a structural
	  refutation (wrong control byte or transmission type) from a
	  physical-plausibility one.

config RADIANT_CORE_SWEEP_DEBUG
	bool "log the search sweep's position once a second"
	default n
	help
	  Bench instrumentation for the discovery path. Once a second, from
	  api_housekeep(): the selected set, the steer queue depth, the dwell
	  credited so far, the sweep and scheduler counters, the pump and
	  housekeeping rates, armed length by slot kind, and how scan chunks
	  ended.

	  It exists because the sweep's RATE is invisible from the host. A sweep
	  running at half speed and a healthy one look identical over the wire -
	  same messages, same responses, no errors - until a device the dongle
	  could hear takes twenty seconds to appear. Two defects were found with
	  exactly these counters and neither showed up in any existing statistic:
	  a chunk-per-set round trip through the event thread, and a set
	  overspending its dwell because the chunk ceiling never came down.

	  Off by default. -DCONFIG_RADIANT_CORE_SWEEP_DEBUG=y on a debug build.

endif # RADIANT_CORE
